/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (4. High Computational Requirements: Advanced speech recognition models often require significant processing power, memory, and storage, making them difficult to implement on low- cost microcontrollers.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (5. Power Consumption: Continuous audio processing and communication with remote servers can increase energy consumption, particularly in battery-powered devices.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (6. Background Noise: Environmental sounds, varying accents, and differences in speaking volume can reduce voice detection accuracy.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (7. Limited Hardware Resources: Small embedded devices have restricted RAM, flash storage, and processing capabilities, which limit the complexity of voice recognition algorithms.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (8. System Reliability: Network failures, microphone quality, and software limitations can affect consistent operation.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 324 0 R
/E (These limitations highlight the need for a low-latency, energy-efficient, and locally operating voice activation system that can deliver reliable performance with reduced internet dependency and computational overhead.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 325 0 R
/Pg 26 0 R
/A [1381 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (14)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 325 0 R
/T (2.3 Research Gap Identified)
/E (2.3 Research Gap Identified)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (Existing voice activation systems have achieved significant progress in speech recognition and human-machine interaction. However, several challenges remain in developing efficient voice activation systems for resource-constrained edge devices. Cloud-based systems often depend on stable internet connectivity and may introduce additional latency due to remote processing. Although edge-based systems reduce network dependency, they must operate within limited memory, processing power, and energy budgets.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (A major research gap is the need to balance low response latency, voice detection accuracy, and computational efficiency within a single embedded system. Some existing solutions focus primarily on recognition accuracy, while others prioritize reduced power consumption or faster processing. Achieving all these requirements simultaneously remains challenging, particularly in noisy environments and on low-cost microcontrollers.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (Furthermore, voice detection performance can vary depending on microphone quality, background noise, speaking patterns, and the algorithm used. There is a need to evaluate system performance using measurable parameters such as response time, detection accuracy, memory utilization, and power consumption.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (The proposed Low Latency and Efficient Voice Activator for Edge Devices project addresses)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (these challenges by implementing local voice command detection using an edge device such as the ESP32-S3. It aims to minimize processing delays, reduce dependence on cloud services, and optimize hardware resource utilization. The project provides a foundation for developing affordable and responsive voice-controlled applications in robotics, smart home automation, and other embedded systems.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
