584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 326 0 R
/T (2.4 Summary  )
/E (2.4 Summary  )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (This chapter presented an overview of existing voice activation systems, their development, limitations, and the research gap identified in current approaches. Voice activation technology has become an important part of modern electronics, embedded systems, and human-machine interaction. Traditional voice recognition systems generally depend on computers or cloud-based servers to process audio data and identify spoken commands. Although these systems provide advanced recognition capabilities, they may experience delays due to network communication and require continuous internet connectivity.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (The literature review highlighted the growing importance of edge computing for developing faster and more efficient voice-controlled applications. Edge devices process data locally, reducing the need to transmit audio information to external servers. Microcontrollers such as the ESP32 and ESP32-S3 provide suitable platforms for implementing lightweight voice activation systems because of their processing capabilities, compact size, and support for embedded applications. Several limitations were identified in existing systems, including high response latency, power consumption, privacy concerns, limited memory, computational complexity, and reduced recognition accuracy in noisy environments. These challenges indicate the need for a system that balances processing speed, detection accuracy, energy efficiency, and hardware resource utilization. The research gap identified in this chapter focuses on developing a cost-effective voice activation solution capable of operating efficiently on resource-constrained edge devices.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
587 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (The proposed Low Latency and Efficient Voice Activator for Edge Devices project aims to address these challenges by implementing local voice command detection and generating appropriate outputs with minimal delay. The system uses an edge computing platform, a microphone, and output indicators such as an LED or OLED display to demonstrate voice activation. Its performance can be evaluated using parameters such as response time, detection accuracy, memory utilization, and power consumption.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
588 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (In conclusion, this chapter establishes the foundation for the proposed system by identifying the limitations of existing approaches and explaining the need for efficient local voice processing. The next chapter discusses the system requirements, hardware components, software tools, and proposed architecture required for implementation.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
